(a) External elected officials shall be recognized at city special events in a manner consistent with the level of government they represent, in the following general order of precedence:
(1) Federal officials;
(2) State officials;
(3) County officials;
(4) Municipal officials (elected leaders from other cities that may be present);
(5) Former elected officials.
(b) If presence at event is known, such officials shall be formally acknowledged during the event program and shall be offered an opportunity to provide remarks, subject to reasonable time constraints established by event organizers.
(c) If at all possible, the inclusion of remarks shall be coordinated in advance to ensure alignment with event objectives and scheduling. Persons including elected officials shall not speak or give remarks unless they are listed in the program. This includes any dual roles that elected officials may hold other than their elected office designation.
(Ord. No. 2026-020, § 5, 7-14-2026)

258:
(a) City special events shall not be used for campaign activity by any individual or entity.
(b) No person shall engage in campaign activity at a city special event, including but not limited to:
(1) Distributing campaign literature or materials;
(2) Displaying campaign signs, apparel, or messaging intended to influence an election;
(3) Soliciting votes, endorsements, or political contributions;
(4) Delivering remarks that advocate for or against any candidate or ballot measure;
(5) Using any photographs from the event to promote their campaign;
(6) Using the city name, logo, or city seal in campaign promoted material.
(c) Former elected officials, including those who are candidates for elected office, are expressly prohibited from using city special events as campaign platforms or incorporating such events into campaign-related communications in a manner that implies city endorsement.
(d) Nothing in this section shall prohibit elected officials from performing official duties or delivering remarks consistent with the purpose of the event, provided such remarks do not constitute campaign activity.
(e) Elected officials shall not engage in photographs and passive or active endorsements while performing official duties at any event or circumstance.
(Ord. No. 2026-020, § 7, 7-14-2026)

